00:03:19.328 --> 00:03:21.882
- [Voiceover] Mariya asks,

00:03:21.882 --> 00:03:24.589
"Do you think too many brands
are on Snapchat Discover?

00:03:24.589 --> 00:03:27.010
"Does each added brand
decrease the value for others?"

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:29.773
- I think each added brand does

00:03:29.773 --> 00:03:32.071
decrease value for the others

00:03:32.071 --> 00:03:33.534
because it's simple supply and demand.

00:03:33.534 --> 00:03:34.486
When you got there--

00:03:34.486 --> 00:03:37.482
I mean, when there was 18 apps on day one

00:03:37.482 --> 00:03:41.243
or 800 or 53 or whatever it
was on day one of the app store

00:03:41.243 --> 00:03:43.727
that was good for those apps,

00:03:43.727 --> 00:03:44.772
they had more attention.

00:03:44.772 --> 00:03:47.628
When there was three channels
on tv back in the day

00:03:47.628 --> 00:03:48.975
that was good for those channels.

00:03:48.975 --> 00:03:50.554
When it was 13 for quite a while,

00:03:50.554 --> 00:03:51.575
it was good for those channels

00:03:51.575 --> 00:03:53.828
and then you had cable and there were 36

00:03:53.828 --> 00:03:55.825
for a good while and that
was good for those channels

00:03:55.825 --> 00:03:57.728
and then we got into DIRECTV and 1000

00:03:57.728 --> 00:03:59.238
and then we got the internet.

00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:02.883
Of course, everything is
important for supply and demand

00:04:02.883 --> 00:04:06.111
when you've got a bigger
percentage of the attention.

00:04:06.111 --> 00:04:09.524
Now, so it's not as good for
the apps or the media companies

00:04:09.524 --> 00:04:11.985
behind them but it's better for,

00:04:11.985 --> 00:04:15.282
potentially better for
the user and Snapchat

00:04:15.282 --> 00:04:17.047
and that's why it's happening, right.

00:04:17.047 --> 00:04:18.974
If you go on Snapchat
Discover back in the day

00:04:18.974 --> 00:04:19.960
and there's all these brands

00:04:19.960 --> 00:04:21.849
and you don't give a
rat's ass of any of them

00:04:21.849 --> 00:04:22.953
well it's not as valuable to you.

00:04:22.953 --> 00:04:24.325
But as it starts bringing kind of

00:04:24.325 --> 00:04:26.433
new things to the equation.

00:04:26.433 --> 00:04:29.074
New subcultures, niches,
things that you're into,

00:04:29.074 --> 00:04:31.525
you'll be excited and so, you know,

00:04:31.525 --> 00:04:34.461
I think that for Snapchat and for users,

00:04:34.461 --> 00:04:36.945
as long as the channels continue to expand

00:04:36.945 --> 00:04:38.803
and bring value to people.

00:04:38.803 --> 00:04:39.662
It's like the internet.

00:04:39.662 --> 00:04:41.403
The internet's better
than cable television.

00:04:41.403 --> 00:04:44.098
Because it gives more people more options

00:04:44.098 --> 00:04:46.029
of consuming what they want.

00:04:46.029 --> 00:04:47.488
Then when we'll counter-argue that,

00:04:47.488 --> 00:04:49.053
well then, there's just a bunch more crap.

00:04:49.053 --> 00:04:51.061
There was plenty of crap
when it was 13 channels.

00:04:51.061 --> 00:04:52.366
There was crappy--

00:04:52.366 --> 00:04:53.945
Snapchat had what, like eight or nine,

00:04:53.945 --> 00:04:55.199
six channels when they launched?

00:04:55.199 --> 00:04:56.406
Did you love all of them?

00:04:56.406 --> 00:04:57.126
- Nope.

00:04:57.126 --> 00:04:58.055
- Did you?

00:04:58.055 --> 00:04:59.263
- No.
- [Gary] No, I mean,

00:04:59.263 --> 00:05:02.759
- So like, this notion that
limitations curate better stuff

00:05:02.759 --> 00:05:05.267
is a very arrogant POV from the people

00:05:05.267 --> 00:05:07.078
that have vested interest in staying small

00:05:07.078 --> 00:05:08.541
'cause they're getting more value.

00:05:08.541 --> 00:05:10.213
I like the capitalism.

00:05:10.213 --> 00:05:12.651
And the real meritocracy.

00:05:12.651 --> 00:05:13.858
It's why I love the internet.

00:05:13.858 --> 00:05:16.458
I love that you watch
or listen to this show

00:05:16.458 --> 00:05:19.941
because you really like
or I'm bringing you value.

00:05:19.941 --> 00:05:22.542
Because there's a shitload of options.

00:05:22.542 --> 00:05:24.864
It's not because it's 1957

00:05:24.864 --> 00:05:28.532
and I'm one of the three
anchormen that evening.

00:05:28.532 --> 00:05:31.110
You know, when there's a
couple of other options.

00:05:31.110 --> 00:05:33.478
I love the supply and
demand of the internet,

00:05:33.478 --> 00:05:36.311
of the open web, of the
maturity of where we are.

00:05:36.311 --> 00:05:38.749
And so, I like that for the end consumer.

00:05:38.749 --> 00:05:40.537
I like that everybody that's
watching and listening

00:05:40.537 --> 00:05:43.160
right now, has unlimited options

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.482
and they can find what brings them value.

00:05:45.482 --> 00:05:48.129
To answer the question directly,

00:05:48.129 --> 00:05:50.172
yeah, it's not as good
for the media properties

00:05:50.172 --> 00:05:52.680
but that doesn't matter
because Snapchat's job

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.670
is to make it better for their
users and for themselves.

00:05:56.813 --> 00:05:58.206
- [Voiceover] DJ Chapman asks,

00:05:58.206 --> 00:06:00.110
"My podcast website gets
a lot of page views

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:01.828
"but they're not translating into listens.

00:06:01.828 --> 00:06:03.872
"What can I do to help conversion?"

00:06:03.872 --> 00:06:06.138
- Well, I mean, there's a lot of 
things you could be doing, DJ.

00:06:06.138 --> 00:06:08.337
One, you need to be thinking
about how you get them in there

00:06:08.339 --> 00:06:10.058
And two, you need to
think about what happens

00:06:10.058 --> 00:06:11.066
when they get there.

00:06:11.066 --> 00:06:12.556
A la, let's go old school.

00:06:12.556 --> 00:06:16.410
Let's make pretend that
your pods are a restaurant.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.964
Okay, you open a restaurant

00:06:18.964 --> 00:06:21.565
and you have thousands of
people coming to your restaurant

00:06:21.565 --> 00:06:23.469
but they're not ordering food,
they're just sitting there

00:06:23.469 --> 00:06:25.001
and they're not bringing you any value.

00:06:25.001 --> 00:06:26.255
There's a couple things to debate.

00:06:26.255 --> 00:06:29.273
One, did you bring the right people there?

00:06:29.273 --> 00:06:32.222
If your marketing was,
come to this restaurant

00:06:32.222 --> 00:06:34.149
and something amazing is gonna happen

00:06:34.149 --> 00:06:35.403
that you've never seen before.

00:06:35.403 --> 00:06:36.610
We can't tell you, it's a big surprise.

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:37.957
You might win a million dollars.

00:06:37.957 --> 00:06:39.490
They all start showing up,

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:41.022
they weren't there to eat your burger.

00:06:41.022 --> 00:06:42.299
They weren't there to eat your fries.

00:06:42.299 --> 00:06:44.064
They weren't there to eat your salad.

00:06:44.064 --> 00:06:45.666
They weren't there to
drink a cup of coffee.

00:06:45.666 --> 00:06:47.639
They were there because you made a promise

00:06:47.639 --> 00:06:49.218
that when you got into the restaurant,

00:06:49.218 --> 00:06:50.890
I'm treating your place
as a diner, by the way,

00:06:50.890 --> 00:06:51.796
in my brain.

00:06:51.796 --> 00:06:53.862
You know, you didn't deliver that.

00:06:53.862 --> 00:06:55.348
They're like cool, pickles and coleslaw

00:06:55.348 --> 00:06:57.577
but I came because I thought
that we were gonna get

00:06:57.577 --> 00:06:58.854
a million dollars.

00:06:58.854 --> 00:07:01.176
There's that or there's another thing.

00:07:01.176 --> 00:07:03.057
They came and when they got there,

00:07:03.057 --> 00:07:06.006
they walked into the
restaurant and there is no--

00:07:06.006 --> 00:07:07.654
There's nobody in the front

00:07:07.654 --> 00:07:09.326
to guide them to their seat.

00:07:09.326 --> 00:07:11.602
There's not maitre d' or a welcome person.

00:07:11.602 --> 00:07:13.947
They walked in and it's
like an empty warehouse.

00:07:13.947 --> 00:07:16.361
With weird signs and they're just confused

00:07:16.361 --> 00:07:18.219
to where to go sit and where to go eat.

00:07:18.219 --> 00:07:20.100
I'm painting you a picture
because there's two fundamental

00:07:20.100 --> 00:07:22.096
things that happen when
you have this problem.

00:07:22.096 --> 00:07:23.559
This is for all of you.

00:07:23.559 --> 00:07:25.928
Either you didn't make the
right promise to get them there

00:07:25.928 --> 00:07:28.041
and when they got there,
they were disappointed

00:07:28.041 --> 00:07:29.271
or not interested

00:07:29.271 --> 00:07:30.246
or it was the wrong reason

00:07:30.246 --> 00:07:31.918
and the only thing you
were trying to make happen

00:07:31.918 --> 00:07:33.009
was get everybody there.

00:07:33.009 --> 00:07:34.495
You didn't think about
the part that mattered

00:07:34.495 --> 00:07:35.621
which was get them to order food.

00:07:35.621 --> 00:07:38.164
Or when they got there because
they wanted to be there,

00:07:38.164 --> 00:07:41.902
they didn't understand how to
execute on the transaction.

00:07:41.902 --> 00:07:44.108
So that means your marketing stinks

00:07:44.108 --> 00:07:45.989
and you're not telling the
proper story of getting them in.

00:07:45.989 --> 00:07:49.913
Or your UI and UX or promise
or landing page optimization

00:07:49.913 --> 00:07:54.162
or directions to do once they're
there, or there is an issue

00:07:54.162 --> 00:07:57.227
Makes me think about how
is it optimized for mobile.

00:07:57.227 --> 00:07:58.920
Maybe moblile's bad.

00:07:58.920 --> 00:07:59.942
It also makes me think,

00:07:59.942 --> 00:08:01.637
and this is more of the kind of problem,

00:08:01.637 --> 00:08:03.123
that the marketing that you're doing

00:08:03.123 --> 00:08:05.491
or the PR or whatever you're
doing to get people there,

00:08:05.491 --> 00:08:06.977
it's predicated on getting them there,

00:08:06.977 --> 00:08:08.719
not to do the action that you want.

00:08:08.719 --> 00:08:11.226
Or number three, they're
getting a very quick sample

00:08:11.226 --> 00:08:13.873
and they're hearing or
seeing you somewhere else

00:08:13.873 --> 00:08:15.313
and they just don't like you.

00:08:15.313 --> 00:08:16.467
Like there's always number three which is,

00:08:16.467 --> 00:08:18.517
you go to the restaurant, you sat down,

00:08:18.517 --> 00:08:19.864
you knew it was gonna be a burger joint.

00:08:19.864 --> 00:08:21.582
You got there, there was
somebody that sat you down.

00:08:21.582 --> 00:08:22.998
You ordered a burger, you ate the burger

00:08:22.998 --> 00:08:24.786
and you're like, "Eh."

00:08:24.786 --> 00:08:26.783
Not that good of a burger, India.

00:08:26.783 --> 00:08:27.967
Like just fine.

00:08:27.967 --> 00:08:30.428
Or more likely, for so many
people that are watching here.

00:08:30.428 --> 00:08:31.961
Good burger,

00:08:31.961 --> 00:08:34.840
but I'm not gonna go 15 blocks,
I got a burger over here.

00:08:34.840 --> 00:08:35.885
Next to me.

00:08:35.885 --> 00:08:38.625
There's a burger one block away.

00:08:38.625 --> 00:08:40.482
You're not a burger.

00:08:40.482 --> 00:08:41.666
(laughs)

00:08:41.666 --> 00:08:43.152
But there's a burger here

00:08:43.152 --> 00:08:44.801
and I don't wanna go there.

00:08:44.801 --> 00:08:46.287
So that's another thing,
that the friction,

00:08:46.287 --> 00:08:48.307
convenience is such king, right.

00:08:48.307 --> 00:08:51.697
They may have somebody else's
app already downloaded.

00:08:51.697 --> 00:08:54.739
Or they already have to
other podcasts downloaded.

00:08:54.739 --> 00:08:56.712
And then they just don't
wanna, they don't wanna even

00:08:56.712 --> 00:08:58.570
take a second to download another one.

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:00.613
They, as a person, back to contradiction

00:09:00.613 --> 00:09:02.285
of the first question, not contradiction,

00:09:02.285 --> 00:09:03.887
different look at it is,

00:09:03.887 --> 00:09:06.580
they don't want more supply of content.

00:09:06.580 --> 00:09:09.367
So like, I've already got
Gary Vee and I've already got

00:09:09.367 --> 00:09:10.853
James Altucher, I've already got Ferris

00:09:10.853 --> 00:09:12.965
or I've already got School
of Greatness or I already--

00:09:12.965 --> 00:09:14.939
Like, I don't need another
one that's the same.

00:09:14.939 --> 00:09:17.418
You're not differentiating.

00:09:18.306 --> 00:09:19.954
That was a real business lesson.

00:09:19.954 --> 00:09:22.114
That was a really good question to answer.

00:09:22.114 --> 00:09:24.111
I really hope people watch that thrice.

00:09:24.111 --> 00:09:25.527
- [India] Cool.

00:09:25.527 --> 00:09:26.990
- [Gary] Cool is right.

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:28.197
- Cool.

00:09:28.197 --> 00:09:30.465
From Brittany.

00:09:30.465 --> 00:09:31.338
- Spears?

00:09:31.338 --> 00:09:32.801
- Yeah, Brittany Spears.
- [Gary] Okay.

00:09:32.801 --> 00:09:34.775
- I'm gonna start letting you
pretend it's famous people.

00:09:34.775 --> 00:09:35.727
- Okay, good.

00:09:35.727 --> 00:09:37.956
- [Voiceover] Brittany asks,

00:09:37.956 --> 00:09:39.790
"How do you personally
take charge in a meeting

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:42.341
"when you feel others
are being disrespectful?"

00:09:42.341 --> 00:09:45.316
- I guess Brittany's asking for herself

00:09:45.316 --> 00:09:46.835
and for everybody who's listening.

00:09:46.835 --> 00:09:49.110
For me, I mean,

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:51.177
I just get involve--
(laughs)

00:09:51.177 --> 00:09:53.754
I mean, first of all 99% of the time,

00:09:53.754 --> 00:09:55.936
the context of the meeting,
I have the leverage.

00:09:55.936 --> 00:09:59.977
Either it's my meeting and/or
I have a lot of authority

00:09:59.977 --> 00:10:02.159
or street cred to open my mouth.

00:10:02.159 --> 00:10:04.110
One more time, how do you
take charge when you think

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:05.572
somebody else is being disrespected?

00:10:05.572 --> 00:10:07.035
- [India] When you feel others
are being disrespectful,

00:10:07.035 --> 00:10:08.243
meaning, if it's uncomfortable--

00:10:08.243 --> 00:10:09.496
- I see this all the time

00:10:09.496 --> 00:10:10.782
and I see people struggling with it

00:10:10.782 --> 00:10:12.010
especially if they're middle management

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:13.769
or the new kid on the block, or an intern.

00:10:13.769 --> 00:10:15.858
But they have the EQ or
the empathy and like,

00:10:15.858 --> 00:10:17.553
this is unfortunate.

00:10:17.553 --> 00:10:21.152
I would say that there's
only two ways to live life.

00:10:21.152 --> 00:10:25.703
To tactfully address things

00:10:25.703 --> 00:10:29.140
or to eat and have regrets
that you didn't address it.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:30.161
What's the name again?

00:10:30.161 --> 00:10:31.044
Brittany.

00:10:31.044 --> 00:10:33.992
Brittany, I think that
you have choices here.

00:10:33.992 --> 00:10:37.731
If I was in a meeting with this crew

00:10:37.731 --> 00:10:40.796
and I was being disrespectful to Staphon

00:10:40.796 --> 00:10:43.512
and India felt like she
wanted to say something.

00:10:43.512 --> 00:10:45.045
What's running through her mind is,

00:10:45.045 --> 00:10:46.484
if I call Gary out here,

00:10:46.484 --> 00:10:48.945
and first of all, she has
a lot of context on me

00:10:48.945 --> 00:10:50.826
so she's probably thinking, oh crap,

00:10:50.826 --> 00:10:53.055
Staphon's doing something
wrong that I don't know about

00:10:53.055 --> 00:10:54.123
'cause Gary's usually right.

00:10:54.123 --> 00:10:56.027
But let's say I was tone deaf
(laughs)

00:10:56.027 --> 00:10:58.628
and I didn't have equity with
her and it was her first week.

00:10:58.628 --> 00:11:01.019
She's thinking that if
she calls me out and says,

00:11:01.019 --> 00:11:01.971
Gary, why are doing--

00:11:01.971 --> 00:11:02.970
You're being rude.

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:04.827
She's thinking, oh crap,
that can get me fired.

00:11:04.827 --> 00:11:06.035
And then, what does it mean to me?

00:11:06.035 --> 00:11:07.846
People are doing practical--

00:11:07.846 --> 00:11:10.562
There's always the pressure
of doing the right thing

00:11:10.562 --> 00:11:11.533
versus the practical thing.

00:11:11.533 --> 00:11:12.629
And then you're always questioning,

00:11:12.629 --> 00:11:15.020
are you good enough to know
what the right thing is.

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:16.599
There's all that stuff.

00:11:16.599 --> 00:11:17.667
I don't know,

00:11:17.667 --> 00:11:20.129
I have had a very successful life.

00:11:20.129 --> 00:11:21.731
Forget about career.

00:11:21.731 --> 00:11:23.565
On being comfortable of addressing things

00:11:23.565 --> 00:11:26.700
in real time, in the room, if it needs to.

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:29.625
My level of thinking of
disrespect is quite high

00:11:29.625 --> 00:11:32.133
because I like combativeness
and competitiveness

00:11:32.133 --> 00:11:34.431
and I've also always had leverage.

00:11:34.431 --> 00:11:35.659
I work for myself.

00:11:35.659 --> 00:11:37.183
So, my advice to myself

00:11:37.183 --> 00:11:39.048
or how I think about the
world is very different

00:11:39.048 --> 00:11:41.188
than the advice I'd
give to a lot of people.

00:11:41.188 --> 00:11:43.580
I think you go with the one strike policy.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:46.505
India should grab me or send me an email

00:11:46.505 --> 00:11:49.779
after that meeting and say,
hey, I felt a hair uncomfortable

00:11:49.779 --> 00:11:52.519
with the way that you were
treating Staphon in that meeting.

00:11:52.519 --> 00:11:53.948
Can we either talk about
it, she'd grab me in person.

00:11:53.948 --> 00:11:55.102
She can send an email.

00:11:55.102 --> 00:11:56.954
I like in person 'cause
no context is lost.

00:11:56.954 --> 00:11:59.136
'Cause if I got that
email from India, I'm like

00:11:59.136 --> 00:12:01.039
that's a little prima
donna for a youngster.

00:12:01.039 --> 00:12:02.194
(laughs)
She doesn't know all the details.

00:12:02.194 --> 00:12:03.376
But if she told it to me,

00:12:03.376 --> 00:12:05.034
I'd be able to feel the energy.

00:12:05.034 --> 00:12:06.659
That's one lesson I'd like
a lot of you to learn.

00:12:06.659 --> 00:12:07.936
Sending a text or sending an email,

00:12:07.936 --> 00:12:09.585
where it's an important moment,

00:12:09.585 --> 00:12:11.164
you're losing so much context.

00:12:11.164 --> 00:12:13.672
The energy, especially if
you go to an EQ person,

00:12:13.672 --> 00:12:16.017
the energy is so powerful when
you can create the context

00:12:16.017 --> 00:12:17.085
so I highly recommend that.

00:12:17.085 --> 00:12:19.871
But I would probably
go with a communication

00:12:19.871 --> 00:12:22.379
that wasn't confrontational
in the room with that manager

00:12:22.379 --> 00:12:23.911
or that boss the first time.

00:12:23.911 --> 00:12:26.883
Behind the scenes,
lightweight, treading water.

00:12:26.883 --> 00:12:29.716
And then, A, seeing how they respond.

00:12:29.716 --> 00:12:31.086
Because I would respond,

00:12:31.086 --> 00:12:32.781
and we've been there and done that, India.

00:12:32.781 --> 00:12:34.395
I would respond favorable
which would make you

00:12:34.395 --> 00:12:36.705
more comfortable and
safe to talk to me again.

00:12:36.705 --> 00:12:39.050
Others will be like,
shut you (blank) mouth.

00:12:39.050 --> 00:12:40.838
And that would make
you not as much comfort

00:12:40.838 --> 00:12:43.647
and then I would address it
in the room the second time.

00:12:43.647 --> 00:12:45.505
If I said, shut your
fuckin' mouth to India

00:12:45.505 --> 00:12:47.316
and then she did it again the second time,

00:12:47.316 --> 00:12:48.523
couple things would happen.

00:12:48.523 --> 00:12:50.450
She'd feel like she was
getting that off her chest.

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:51.402
More importantly, I'd be like,

00:12:51.402 --> 00:12:53.515
damn, she really does care about this.

00:12:53.515 --> 00:12:55.303
It's just life.

00:12:55.303 --> 00:12:57.021
Doing the right thing is
always the right thing.

00:12:57.021 --> 00:12:59.088
You just gotta make sure
you're doing the right thing.

00:12:59.088 --> 00:13:01.038
Way to many people romantically

00:13:01.038 --> 00:13:02.617
wanna fight against the system,

00:13:02.617 --> 00:13:03.778
fight against the boss,

00:13:03.778 --> 00:13:05.427
fight against the company.

00:13:05.427 --> 00:13:07.424
And I've had people in this organization

00:13:07.424 --> 00:13:09.629
that have barked up the wrong tree.

00:13:09.629 --> 00:13:10.953
Because they've worked in other places

00:13:10.953 --> 00:13:12.578
where the person doesn't give a crap

00:13:12.578 --> 00:13:13.878
and doesn't turn every stone

00:13:13.878 --> 00:13:16.015
and doesn't have a ton of context.

00:13:16.015 --> 00:13:19.892
That is something you
need to be careful of.

00:13:19.892 --> 00:13:22.400
Do not walk into a buzzsaw
because you do have a manager

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:24.954
or boss that actually knows
what they're talking about.

00:13:24.954 --> 00:13:28.646
Now, if you're great at EQ
and the tone and the taste,

00:13:28.646 --> 00:13:29.992
roll, let roll.

00:13:29.992 --> 00:13:31.455
But this is not a very simple question.

00:13:31.455 --> 00:13:32.918
There's a lot of angles, as you could see,

00:13:32.918 --> 00:13:34.961
in two quick seconds
that I've given you here,

00:13:34.961 --> 00:13:36.494
it's a lot of context building.

00:13:36.494 --> 00:13:38.607
Who are you standing up for?

00:13:38.607 --> 00:13:41.068
I mean, the amount of
times that people here

00:13:41.068 --> 00:13:45.062
have stood up for somebody
who's straight losing,

00:13:45.062 --> 00:13:46.803
doing the wrong thing,

00:13:46.803 --> 00:13:47.848
but they're homies.

00:13:47.848 --> 00:13:50.007
Matt, let's talk to you
because they hear it from this.

00:13:50.007 --> 00:13:50.936
- Okay.

00:13:50.936 --> 00:13:52.190
- We have a tremendous culture here

00:13:52.190 --> 00:13:55.139
where, obviously, I'd like to say that

00:13:55.139 --> 00:13:58.459
but how many friends do
you have in this company?

00:13:58.459 --> 00:14:01.013
People you actually hang
out with outside of work?

00:14:01.013 --> 00:14:02.127
- A solid amount.

00:14:02.127 --> 00:14:02.917
- Give me a number.

00:14:02.917 --> 00:14:04.217
- 15, 20.

00:14:04.217 --> 00:14:05.843
- Great, so first of all,

00:14:05.843 --> 00:14:07.932
everybody who at Vayner's
watching this is now wondering,

00:14:07.932 --> 00:14:09.267
wait a minute, am I number
21 and what the hell.

00:14:09.267 --> 00:14:10.267
- Sorry guys.

00:14:10.267 --> 00:14:11.462
- I thought we were friends.

00:14:11.462 --> 00:14:12.344
(laughs)

00:14:12.344 --> 00:14:14.457
15 to 20, I think anybody who's watching

00:14:14.457 --> 00:14:16.508
we would all recognize
and that's a big number.

00:14:16.508 --> 00:14:17.947
There's a lot of people watching here

00:14:17.947 --> 00:14:19.248
who don't have single friend.

00:14:19.248 --> 00:14:20.966
Outside, everybody just--

00:14:20.966 --> 00:14:22.613
If you, not if,

00:14:22.613 --> 00:14:24.472
when your friend, one of those 15 to 20

00:14:24.472 --> 00:14:27.444
complains about Vayner,
it's impossible for you

00:14:27.444 --> 00:14:29.000
not to take their side,
they're your friend.

00:14:29.000 --> 00:14:29.882
- Exactly.

00:14:29.882 --> 00:14:31.136
- I mean, that's an impossible game.

00:14:31.136 --> 00:14:31.995
- Absolutely.

00:14:31.995 --> 00:14:33.876
- And I assume, I'm asking you now.

00:14:33.876 --> 00:14:36.221
Even though you like me
and think I'm a good guy

00:14:36.221 --> 00:14:37.823
and it's a good company, good culture.

00:14:37.823 --> 00:14:42.397
It's so much easier to have
Janet's back than the company's.

00:14:42.397 --> 00:14:43.953
- Sure.

00:14:43.953 --> 00:14:47.273
- That right there, is
the issue at hand, right.

00:14:47.273 --> 00:14:49.038
You might be standing up for somebody.

00:14:49.038 --> 00:14:50.895
Have you ever wanted to
stand up for somebody?

00:14:50.895 --> 00:14:51.708
- [Matt] Yeah.

00:14:51.708 --> 00:14:52.776
- Have you?

00:14:52.776 --> 00:14:54.239
- [Matt] Sometimes yes and sometimes no.

00:14:54.239 --> 00:14:55.632
- Right, and so

00:14:55.632 --> 00:14:57.280
it's just tough 'cause
you don't know every--

00:14:57.280 --> 00:14:59.997
I mean, I know a lot of the
friend pods in this company.

00:14:59.997 --> 00:15:01.232
I knew to ask that question

00:15:01.232 --> 00:15:02.661
'cause I knew it was a good outcome

00:15:02.661 --> 00:15:04.897
because I know what's
going on here, right.

00:15:04.897 --> 00:15:06.847
Even people that are a little quiet

00:15:06.847 --> 00:15:08.886
or what have you are finding friends.

00:15:08.886 --> 00:15:10.050
It's amazing, right.

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:11.873
We got a good thing going.

00:15:11.873 --> 00:15:14.834
The danger of that is blind
support to your homies

00:15:14.834 --> 00:15:16.784
versus what's going on in the office.

00:15:16.784 --> 00:15:17.806
There are people here

00:15:17.806 --> 00:15:19.037
who are the greatest human--

00:15:19.037 --> 00:15:20.964
I literally want to adopt them.

00:15:20.964 --> 00:15:22.357
I literally want to adopt them,

00:15:22.357 --> 00:15:23.471
hey come in my family.

00:15:23.471 --> 00:15:24.470
I love you that much.

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:26.397
Who are average workers.

00:15:26.397 --> 00:15:28.371
That's just real-life shit.

00:15:29.981 --> 00:15:31.635
To think if I was their homie,

00:15:31.635 --> 00:15:33.284
outside of work and had all the feelings

00:15:33.284 --> 00:15:37.231
of the humanity that is
them which is remarkable.

00:15:37.771 --> 00:15:39.605
I want to adopt them.

00:15:39.605 --> 00:15:40.882
And to think about them complaining

00:15:40.882 --> 00:15:42.020
or struggle, why didn't I get promoted,

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:43.227
my boss is not taking care of me,

00:15:43.227 --> 00:15:44.806
this and that and the other thing.

00:15:44.806 --> 00:15:47.198
It's impossible for the
other 550 people here

00:15:47.198 --> 00:15:49.497
intermingled with each other
not to support that person.

00:15:49.497 --> 00:15:51.447
They're the best.

00:15:51.447 --> 00:15:53.676
But I have the optics of
another thing which is

00:15:53.676 --> 00:15:54.999
the black and white.

00:15:54.999 --> 00:15:56.323
Not the warm and fuzzy,

00:15:56.323 --> 00:15:57.925
the do you have skill.

00:15:57.925 --> 00:15:59.318
I'm the greatest guy of all time.

00:15:59.318 --> 00:16:02.708
I don't think LeBron wants
me on his basketball team.

00:16:02.708 --> 00:16:04.217
I don't have the raw skills

00:16:04.217 --> 00:16:07.654
to provide him value for
what he's trying to achieve.

00:16:07.654 --> 00:16:09.326
I'm the best.

00:16:09.326 --> 00:16:10.370
And if he wants to do business

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:12.553
I'll make him more money
than he can even realize.

00:16:12.553 --> 00:16:15.804
Even more than he makes, which
is more than he can realize.

00:16:15.804 --> 00:16:17.313
And that's the game.

00:16:17.313 --> 00:16:19.217
And so, that's the other
part of the equation.

00:16:19.217 --> 00:16:21.539
You gonna step up for the
greatest person of all time, cool.

00:16:21.539 --> 00:16:22.909
You just might get caught

00:16:22.909 --> 00:16:24.998
because they're actually below average

00:16:24.998 --> 00:16:25.974
or not doing a good job.

00:16:25.974 --> 00:16:27.622
Or they may actually act differently,

00:16:27.622 --> 00:16:28.783
I mean, this is happening here too.

00:16:28.783 --> 00:16:30.989
There are people that,
outside of these four walls,

00:16:30.989 --> 00:16:32.591
I want to adopt.

00:16:32.591 --> 00:16:34.704
But when you watch them, how they act

00:16:34.704 --> 00:16:35.818
within a work environment,

00:16:35.818 --> 00:16:37.606
they're just okay.

00:16:37.606 --> 00:16:38.907
Lot a sweeties.

00:16:38.907 --> 00:16:40.462
And then a little snarky and manipulative

00:16:40.462 --> 00:16:42.668
and political in the building.

00:16:42.668 --> 00:16:44.433
That's just real life.

00:16:44.433 --> 00:16:46.964
So know who you're standing up for.

00:16:46.964 --> 00:16:48.868
Interesting shit.

00:16:48.868 --> 00:16:49.750
- Got really deep.

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:50.748
- Yeah, it got very deep.

00:16:50.748 --> 00:16:52.327
That's something we can all learn from.

00:16:52.327 --> 00:16:53.883
I've learned that lesson.

00:16:53.883 --> 00:16:56.065
Like, I know this guy, he's the greatest.

00:16:56.065 --> 00:16:57.784
And like wait a minute,
he sucks in the store.

00:16:57.784 --> 00:17:00.732
I saw with my own
friends that work for me.

00:17:00.732 --> 00:17:03.751
So, anyway.

00:17:03.751 --> 00:17:04.865
- [India] It's intense.

00:17:04.865 --> 00:17:05.864
- It is intense.

00:17:05.864 --> 00:17:07.373
It's intense because

00:17:07.373 --> 00:17:09.672
it's so, this is where
judging where you work

00:17:09.672 --> 00:17:12.666
or who you work for is the key.

00:17:12.666 --> 00:17:15.685
And understand what
they're good and bad at.

00:17:15.685 --> 00:17:18.215
Meaning, you could have a great manager,

00:17:18.215 --> 00:17:19.492
top, top, manager.

00:17:19.492 --> 00:17:20.608
And they might be strong at X

00:17:20.608 --> 00:17:21.978
but they might bad at Y.

00:17:21.978 --> 00:17:23.487
And if they're bad at Y,

00:17:23.487 --> 00:17:24.694
you need to context that.

00:17:24.694 --> 00:17:27.340
There's no blanket statement,
even on the person.

00:17:27.340 --> 00:17:28.571
I have a lot of points of view

00:17:28.571 --> 00:17:29.501
on your strengths and weaknesses

00:17:29.501 --> 00:17:30.824
from a lot of different people.

00:17:30.824 --> 00:17:32.055
Way more than you'd ever think.

00:17:32.055 --> 00:17:33.077
- [Matt] Oh, I'm sure.

00:17:33.077 --> 00:17:36.559
- In a good way, meaning it's
why we're so calculated here

00:17:36.559 --> 00:17:37.697
of what we do.

00:17:37.697 --> 00:17:40.042
'Cause we don't take the
main boss's point of view

00:17:40.042 --> 00:17:40.948
on somebody.

00:17:40.948 --> 00:17:42.480
It's 360.

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:44.106
It's contemporaries, it's
friends outside of work.

00:17:44.106 --> 00:17:45.406
It's people that never heard of him.

00:17:45.406 --> 00:17:46.753
It's people that work for you.

00:17:46.753 --> 00:17:48.169
You can't just be like, oh the boss--

00:17:48.169 --> 00:17:50.305
If you let that, they're
just manipulate what's

00:17:50.305 --> 00:17:51.675
in their best interest.

00:17:51.675 --> 00:17:53.532
'Cause they're just human,
it's not their fault.

00:17:53.532 --> 00:17:55.158
But I think that's what
makes our place tick

00:17:55.158 --> 00:17:56.667
because people have
seen very senior people

00:17:56.667 --> 00:17:58.687
not win the battle against
very junior people.

00:17:58.687 --> 00:18:00.684
Then that's like whoa,

00:18:00.684 --> 00:18:02.054
and that's cool.

00:18:02.054 --> 00:18:03.563
I don't know how I got on that tangent.

00:18:03.563 --> 00:18:04.469
I know how,

00:18:04.469 --> 00:18:06.465
If you wanna step up
for somebody in the room

00:18:06.465 --> 00:18:09.739
you better know all the scores.

00:18:09.739 --> 00:18:10.877
The conversation.

00:18:10.877 --> 00:18:11.968
The skills.

00:18:11.968 --> 00:18:13.292
All the people involved.

00:18:13.292 --> 00:18:14.894
All of it.

00:18:14.894 --> 00:18:15.730
Did she comment on that--

00:18:15.730 --> 00:18:18.609
- Hey Gary, Sam Attard here
from Happy Healthy Human.

00:18:18.609 --> 00:18:20.629
I'm curious, what's the psychology behind

00:18:20.629 --> 00:18:22.579
why people are on Periscope.

00:18:22.579 --> 00:18:24.274
I saw your interview with
Marie Forleo recently

00:18:24.274 --> 00:18:26.097
where talked about the psychology

00:18:26.097 --> 00:18:28.512
of why people are on
Pinterest and Twitter.

00:18:28.512 --> 00:18:30.114
So why Periscope?
- [Gary] Great question.

00:18:30.114 --> 00:18:31.252
Thanks hun.

00:18:31.252 --> 00:18:32.436
- Oh, thanks hun.

00:18:32.436 --> 00:18:33.365
You're welcome Samantha.

00:18:33.365 --> 00:18:34.224
I like the hun.

00:18:34.224 --> 00:18:35.385
(laughs)

00:18:35.385 --> 00:18:37.033
I think the psychology on that is,

00:18:37.033 --> 00:18:38.496
you know, it's live tv right.

00:18:38.496 --> 00:18:41.004
And there's some people
that are good at QVC

00:18:41.004 --> 00:18:42.722
or Saturday Night Live.

00:18:42.722 --> 00:18:44.069
I always got a lot of kick out of,

00:18:44.069 --> 00:18:46.135
as I started meeting famous people,

00:18:46.135 --> 00:18:49.525
I heard an ongoing conversation
of the actors and actresses

00:18:49.525 --> 00:18:51.429
that would not do Curb Your Enthusiasm

00:18:51.429 --> 00:18:53.217
because it was not structured enough.

00:18:53.217 --> 00:18:55.817
They were scared of the
improv nature or SNL.

00:18:55.817 --> 00:18:57.652
And of course, you know, that spoke to me

00:18:57.652 --> 00:19:00.043
because I think I can crush
those things but if you--

00:19:00.043 --> 00:19:01.251
If there was cue cards right now,

00:19:01.251 --> 00:19:03.085
I can't read or spell or I'd be like,

00:19:03.085 --> 00:19:06.428
"And today on the Ask Gary Vee Show."

00:19:06.428 --> 00:19:08.123
You know, I'd be weird.

00:19:08.123 --> 00:19:09.331
So I'm always fascinated

00:19:09.331 --> 00:19:10.701
by people's strengths and weaknesses.

00:19:10.701 --> 00:19:13.208
I think Periscope is great
for people that can improv,

00:19:13.208 --> 00:19:14.648
just roll, keep it interesting.

00:19:14.648 --> 00:19:17.366
And I think people wanna
project themselves.

00:19:17.366 --> 00:19:19.177
I think everybody's
fascinated with television

00:19:19.177 --> 00:19:20.617
and getting out there.

00:19:20.617 --> 00:19:22.057
And so, if you're doing Periscope,

00:19:22.057 --> 00:19:24.425
you're somebody who doesn't
fully take themselves seriously

00:19:24.425 --> 00:19:28.233
and/or you're very confident
in your improv and your truths.

00:19:28.233 --> 00:19:30.485
The reason I'm super into doing DailyVee

00:19:30.485 --> 00:19:31.948
and things of that nature is

00:19:31.948 --> 00:19:34.045
I'm not scared how it's gonna be edited.

00:19:34.045 --> 00:19:36.257
The only reason I'm even
involved in editing is

00:19:36.257 --> 00:19:39.136
sensitive information of clients
and stuff of that nature.

00:19:39.136 --> 00:19:41.318
But clearly, the bald spot thing,

00:19:41.318 --> 00:19:42.967
I'll talk about anything.

00:19:42.967 --> 00:19:44.360
I'm willing to go--

00:19:44.360 --> 00:19:46.334
It's so crazy, I think I
might lose another 15 pounds

00:19:46.334 --> 00:19:47.611
and gain a couple more muscles.

00:19:47.611 --> 00:19:50.374
I'm getting close to
just living life naked.

00:19:50.374 --> 00:19:52.138
(laughs)

00:19:52.138 --> 00:19:53.462
Don't do that, okay.

00:19:53.462 --> 00:19:54.878
Terrible reaction.

00:19:54.878 --> 00:19:55.993
(laughs)

00:19:55.993 --> 00:19:57.200
That's where I'm willing--

00:19:57.200 --> 00:19:59.058
You have to understand.

00:19:59.058 --> 00:20:00.381
Let's use naked as a metaphor.

00:20:00.381 --> 00:20:02.564
Yes, I'll probably not
walk around naked though,

00:20:02.564 --> 00:20:04.143
it's crossed my mind.

00:20:04.143 --> 00:20:05.907
Living life pretty naked.

00:20:05.907 --> 00:20:07.092
And by the way, I'm not naive.

00:20:07.092 --> 00:20:08.136
We've all got skeletons.

00:20:08.136 --> 00:20:09.413
There's plenty things that I don't share.

00:20:09.413 --> 00:20:11.294
You guys all know, I
don't share my family.

00:20:11.294 --> 00:20:14.336
There's a lot but boy,
I like pushing myself

00:20:14.336 --> 00:20:16.542
further and further
because it is literally

00:20:16.542 --> 00:20:18.051
the last battle of a mile.

00:20:18.051 --> 00:20:19.723
When you take ownership

00:20:19.723 --> 00:20:21.766
of all your flaws and weaknesses,

00:20:21.766 --> 00:20:23.368
you've won.

00:20:23.368 --> 00:20:26.874
I actually think that's the
Nirvana of the connected world.

00:20:26.874 --> 00:20:28.964
Yes, privacy's getting pushed on.

00:20:28.964 --> 00:20:30.845
Yes, they'll be negatives.

00:20:30.845 --> 00:20:32.377
But boy, there's a lot of positives

00:20:32.377 --> 00:20:35.070
with us all putting our
laundry to each other.

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:36.092
We get accepted.

00:20:36.092 --> 00:20:38.530
It's no different than
high school to college.

00:20:38.530 --> 00:20:40.341
The difference, guys this is so basic.

00:20:40.341 --> 00:20:41.688
The difference between
high school and college

00:20:41.688 --> 00:20:43.406
and why everybody likes
college, not everybody but

00:20:43.406 --> 00:20:45.496
a lot of people like college more is

00:20:45.496 --> 00:20:48.073
you've matured into a place
where you can own your shit more

00:20:48.073 --> 00:20:50.650
You're sensitive to your
zits and you weird thing.

00:20:50.650 --> 00:20:53.855
There's a lot of weird shit
that happens between 14 and 18.

00:20:53.855 --> 00:20:55.433
We change a lot.

00:20:55.433 --> 00:20:57.221
And we're not ready to calibrate that.

00:20:57.221 --> 00:20:58.892
It takes very far and few between people

00:20:58.892 --> 00:21:00.261
that can own it all the way.

00:21:00.261 --> 00:21:01.447
We're insecure.

00:21:01.447 --> 00:21:03.537
Our security is much stronger

00:21:03.537 --> 00:21:06.230
in our 20's than it is in our teens

00:21:06.230 --> 00:21:08.413
and that's why we like it better.

00:21:09.923 --> 00:21:11.509
- [Voiceover] Adam asks,

00:21:11.509 --> 00:21:12.902
